:BPMNLabel>\\n          <dc:Bounds x=\\"833\\" y=\\"98\\" width=\\"0\\" height=\\"13\\" />\\n        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"MessageFlow_0u4d8v4_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"MessageFlow_0u4d8v4\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"978\\" y=\\"-50\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"978\\" y=\\"196\\" />\\n        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n          <dc:Bounds x=\\"923\\" y=\\"98.5\\" width=\\"0\\" height=\\"13\\" />\\n        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"MessageFlow_16eeqy7_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"MessageFlow_16eeqy7\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1218\\" y=\\"413\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1218\\" y=\\"-50\\" />\\n        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n          <dc:Bounds x=\\"1163\\" y=\\"206.5\\" width=\\"0\\" height=\\"13\\" />\\n        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"MessageFlow_0fxco5w_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"MessageFlow_0fxco5w\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1306\\" y=\\"-50\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1306\\" y=\\"413\\" />\\n        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n          <dc:Bounds x=\\"1251\\" y=\\"207\\" width=\\"0\\" height=\\"13\\" />\\n        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>\\n  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>\\n</bpmn:definitions>\\n", "bpmn_model_description": "The diagram models how a firm acquires a new client by coordinating its Marketing and Management units and interacting with the Potential‑Client pool.  It starts with the Management task “New client needed”, after which the Marketing lane forks in parallel: “Prepare portfolio presentation” and “Analyze market”.  When both are finished, Marketing sends an “offer” to the client (an Intermediate Throw Event) and waits for an “offer response” (Intermediate Catch Event).  The client’s own subprocess receives the offer, analyses it (task “analyze offer”), throws a reply, may request changes (message to “detail offer according to the client”), and later sends a final response.  Back in the firm, Management is informed of the client’s replies, analyses them, and negotiates.  A decision gateway determines whether the negotiation is successful: on the “success” branch the firm prepares a contract, signs it and ends the process; on the “unsuccess” branch it analyses the reasons, records lessons learned and ends.  Throughout, message flows keep the offer‑exchange and response loops synchronized between the firm’s processes and the external client participant."}
