 />\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"DataInputAssociation_0848l82_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"DataInputAssociation_0848l82\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"348\\" y=\\"225\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"924\\" y=\\"580\\" />\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"DataInputAssociation_1pba18q_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"DataInputAssociation_1pba18q\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"348\\" y=\\"230\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"923\\" y=\\"725\\" />\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n      <bpmndi:BPMNEdge id=\\"DataOutputAssociation_1t4jg6p_di\\" bpmnElement=\\"DataOutputAssociation_1t4jg6p\\">\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1325\\" y=\\"71\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1482\\" y=\\"75\\" />\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>\\n  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>\\n</bpmn:definitions>\\n", "bpmn_model_description": "The diagram models a hotel room‑service order from the moment the request is taken to the guest’s doorstep. The Room‑Service Manager (first lane) starts the process by taking down the order, records it, and immediately debits the guest’s account. The flow then splits in a parallel gateway into three concurrent branches: (1) the order is assigned to a Waiter; (2) a ticket is sent to the Kitchen; and (3) an exclusive decision checks whether any alcoholic beverages were ordered. If alcoholic drinks are required, the manager hands the order to the Sommelier, who follows an inclusive gateway that may both fetch wine from the cellar and/or prepare other alcoholic drinks; the two paths re‑join before an exclusive gateway that synchronises with the Kitchen’s “Prepare Meal” activity. If no alcoholic drinks are ordered, a short “note preference for non‑alcoholic drinks” task runs and then joins the same exclusive gateway. Meanwhile, the Waiter’s branch proceeds: after the order is assigned, the waiter readies a service cart, then an exclusive decision asks whether non‑alcoholic drinks are required; if so a “Prepare Non‑Alcoholic Drinks” task is executed, otherwise the path skips it. All parallel branches (prepared meal, any drinks, and the ready cart) converge at a final parallel gateway, after which the Waiter delivers the complete order to the guest’s room and the process ends."}
