239245,-0.0049171243,0.039232597,-0.0066866623,0.040151294,0.035098452,-0.002080121,0.007970752,0.047396485,-0.0009780745,0.021965243,0.034555584,0.0097611705,-0.025305964,-0.05065369,-0.015826667,-0.038815007,-0.0005976759,-0.03017089,0.006577045,0.021349298,0.012120554,0.039357875,0.032467633,-0.0077515175,0.02637082,-0.06689794,0.012245831,-0.041424945,0.014636535,-0.0018869855,-0.040568884,0.03225884,0.0012123164,-0.031256624,-0.010048264,-0.012893097,0.052115254,0.01908387,0.0063473703,-0.034994055,-0.014093668,-0.00424898,0.0033276717,0.024679579,-0.041863415,0.031131346,-0.031110467,-0.036560018,-0.009442758,-0.011410651,-0.042635955,-0.029126914,-0.0062220935,0.013623879,0.009708972,0.043137062,0.009046047,-0.014469499,-0.043137062,-0.029815938,0.017246474]	The process starts with an informational meeting; if the meeting is judged unsuccessful the workflow ends, otherwise the team creates a storyboard and then splits into two parallel streams. In the first stream the designers work on the design and hand it over to development, while in the second stream the content team prepares the site content, drafts an initial version and, after a “review needed?” decision, either proceeds directly to the final‑version preparation or sends the draft to the marketing team for a review of the initial draft before returning to the final‑version step. Both streams reconvene at a parallel‑join that leads to a building activity, followed by a technical review and a site‑approval step. An “approved?” decision determines whether the site is sent back to rebuilding (loop) or the process finishes successfully. The primary participants are the Web design team (handling design, development, content, building, review and approval) and the marketing team (responsible for reviewing the initial draft).	{"id": "WebDesign_v2", "source": "/app/bpmn_examples/CoherenceCheckingDataset/Web Design/WebDesign_v2.bpmn", "bpmn_model_xml": "<?xml version=\\"1.0\\" encoding=\\"UTF-8\\"?>\\n<bpmn:def