>\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1030\\" y=\\"446\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1030\\" y=\\"510\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1440\\" y=\\"510\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"1440\\" y=\\"680\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"766\\" y=\\"680\\" />\\n        <di:waypoint x=\\"766\\" y=\\"461\\" />\\n        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n          <dc:Bounds x=\\"1449\\" y=\\"592\\" width=\\"13\\" height=\\"14\\" />\\n        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>\\n      </bpmndi:BPMNEdge>\\n    </bpmndi:BPMNPlane>\\n  </bpmndi:BPMNDiagram>\\n</bpmn:definitions>\\n", "bpmn_model_description": "The “Recourse Clerk” lane starts when a probable recourse is detected (message start). The clerk first checks the case and, via an exclusive gateway, decides whether a recourse is actually possible; if not, the case is closed immediately. When a recourse is possible, the clerk sends a request for payment and then a reminder, after which an event‑based gateway waits for one of three events: (1) a disagreement letter, which triggers a reasoning check; if the reasoning is accepted the case is closed, otherwise it is handed over to a collection agency and left open; (2) a timer indicating the reminder is due, which leads to a decision gateway that counts reminders – a second reminder loops back to send another reminder, while a “reminded twice” outcome also hands the case to the collection agency; (3) a payment receipt, which causes the clerk to make a booking and then close the case. In all paths the process ends either with the case closed (after successful payment, accepted reasoning, or no recourse) or with the case handed to a collection agency and marked open."}
